Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:27:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 219/221] feat(area): several distributors per entity, each
 with its own offset

CDistributorComponent is no longer Singleton. One spline routinely wants a fence
on the line, lamp posts two metres to the side and bollards a metre further out,
and each of those is an independent generator with its own meshes, seed,
spacing, offset and render nodes reading the same shape. The shape components
stay Singleton - decision 01's "one shape per entity" is untouched.

Nothing had to change in the editor for it. CreateComponentWidgets already keys
each panel by (component type, instance index) - the folded-GUID key plus
componentTypeIndex, EntityObject.cpp:1117-1120 - and the edit-tool payload
carries the component's INSTANCE guid, so Bake To Brushes resolves through
GetComponentByGUID and bakes the distributor whose button was pressed and no
other. Each instance keeps its own m_instances, m_renderNodes and rebuild
signature, so they neither share nodes nor trigger each other's rebuilds.

Three members make several of them useful rather than merely possible:

- Offset (Vec3, in the CURVE's frame: X along the tangent, Y sideways along the
  normal, Z up) is the base offset every instance gets before the existing
  jitter. Expressing it in the curve frame rather than the entity's is the point:
  a distributor pushed sideways holds that distance all the way round a bend.
- Along Shift (metres) slides the whole sample pattern along the curve, so two
  distributors with the same spacing interleave instead of landing on top of each
  other. An open curve DROPS the instances that slide past an end - clamping
  would pile every one of them onto the last position - and a closed curve wraps
  them round, because a ring has no ends to fall off.
- Label is pushed onto IEntityComponent::SetName, which is what titles the
  inspector panel (EntityObject.cpp:1078-1085), so three distributors are three
  readable panels instead of three called "Distributor". The rebuild log line
  names it too.

Rotation Offset was deliberately NOT added as a fourth member: Z Angle already is
exactly that - a constant turn of every instance about its own up axis, applied
before the jitter. It is relabelled rather than duplicated, because two knobs
doing one job is how a component becomes unpredictable.

